# `core::primitives::booleans` -- `bool`'s declaration site.
#
# One bit of information, stored in one byte. Its operators are `==`/`!=`,
# `&`/`|`/`^`, the short-circuiting `&&`/`||`, and unary `!`. `&`/`|`/`^`
# evaluate both operands; `&&`/`||` evaluate the right one only when the
# left does not already decide the answer, so the spelling is what tells a
# reader whether a call on the right-hand side runs.
#
# Ordering (`<`), arithmetic (`+`), the shifts and `~` are all rejected:
# `bool` has no `arithmetic_repr`, so unlike pointers it never
# decays to an integer to make an operator work. It is a truth value, not a
# zero-or-one.
primitive bool { }
